# 10. Impact Analysis

Successful exploitation allows an attacker to:

* Replace trusted JavaScript resources.
* Execute arbitrary JavaScript in victim browsers.
* Hijack authenticated sessions.
* Steal cookies and CSRF tokens.
* Modify application behavior.
* Deliver phishing interfaces.
* Compromise every user receiving the poisoned cached response.

Because the cache is shared, a single successful poisoning operation may affect numerous users until the cache entry expires.

---

# 11. Root Cause

The vulnerability exists because:

* The application trusted client-controlled forwarding headers intended only for reverse proxies.
* These headers influenced security-sensitive functionality.
* The cache key did not include these headers.
* Cached redirects were shared among all users.

This combination enabled cache poisoning and malicious resource substitution.

---

# 12. Remediation

### High Priority

#### Ignore Client-Controlled Proxy Headers

Only trusted reverse proxies should be permitted to set forwarding headers.

---

#### Validate Forwarded Hosts

Accept only allow-listed hostnames.

---

#### Validate Forwarded Scheme

Reject arbitrary values and only trust internally generated protocol information.

---

#### Improve Cache Key Configuration

Any header influencing generated responses must be included in the cache key or excluded from cacheable responses.

---

#### Disable Caching of Dynamic Redirects

Redirects generated using request metadata should not be cached by shared intermediaries.

---

#### Deploy Content Security Policy (CSP)

Restrict script execution to trusted origins to reduce the impact of malicious JavaScript injection.

