# 14. Conclusion

The assessment identified a **Web Cache Poisoning** vulnerability caused by the unsafe use of the **X-Forwarded-Scheme** and **X-Forwarded-Host** headers.

An attacker could manipulate these untrusted headers to poison the shared cache with a redirect to an attacker-controlled JavaScript resource. Since the poisoned response was subsequently served to other users, arbitrary JavaScript execution became possible within victims' browsers.

This vulnerability demonstrates the security risks associated with trusting proxy headers without proper validation and highlights the importance of secure cache key design and strict origin validation.
